A||N||58||DreXteR||Custom intake||||5||
Z||000000||DreXteR||09-28-2001||04:50 PM||||I want to make or get a custom air intake. There is someone there? I saw the area51 from west coast but that isnt what I have in mind. Any suggestion??<P>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroMaster88||09-28-2001||04:54 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/MY_engine.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P8060004.jpg">  <P>That what you have in mind?<BR>We took the air intake canaster apart....took out the middle part, put the ends around the K&N and put it back together.  I plan on getting a K&N cone filter soon, and it should bolt right on the end.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">               <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!<p>[This message has been edited by FieroMaster88 (edited 09-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||09-28-2001||06:25 PM||||What a great place to suck in water from the vents!<P>EEEK!<P>We don't get that stuff from the sky called rain much around here but that would still be my first worry! Or are you going to use the 84 vent plates?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-28-2001||07:15 PM||||Just as bad or worse... now the engine is breathing air from the engine bay that can easily run 150-200F and maybe more....<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTMike||09-28-2001||07:27 PM||||this is what i did.  got some 3" diamater exaust pipe for free at mufler shop. its about 13" long. put a cone filter on it and then stuck it in the end of the original rubber piece.  the filter now sits just inside the air intake vent on the side of the car but outside the engine compartment  it has a str8 shot of cool outside air in to the t-body.   and man dose that intake scream when i'm flooring it.  at about 120mph at 4 grand its sounds soo scarry but in a good way. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroMaster88||09-28-2001||11:35 PM||||Ok...well I put a K&N filter on there..they SHED WATER...so that is no worry.  Second, the intake tube probably gets pretty hot with the exhaust running underneath it....so i am sure you dont get very COLD air going into it.  Plus..when you have the car moving a lot of cold air gets pulled around those vents...so hot air is not that big of a deal anyway.  Besides, if you put it down were the air box is you are gonna pull up dirt, water and mud from the tirs and road as you drive...so how is that any better?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">              <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000006||West Coast Fiero||09-28-2001||11:46 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroMaster88:<BR><B>Ok...well I put a K&N filter on there..they SHED WATER...<P>you may shed it, but only the water thats not getting sucked through the filter elements<P><BR>so that is no worry.  Second, the intake tube probably gets pretty hot with the exhaust running underneath it<P> the heat coefficient for rubber versus metal is very different, the rubber tube will retain much more heat, thereby causing it to contract with the airflow as it gets hotter thereby restricting airflow, as well as letting the engine breath a hot air charge<P><BR>....so i am sure you dont get very COLD air going into it.  Plus..when you have the car moving a lot of cold air gets pulled around those vents <P><BR>when the car is at freeway speed there is a constant vacum behind the rear glass extending nearly a foot, thereby allowing no air into the engine bay, but rather letting the hot air out as it was designed to do<P><BR>...so hot air is not that big of a deal anyway.  Besides, if you put it down were the air box is you are gonna pull up dirt, water and mud from the tirs and road as you drive...<P><BR>if this was the case, GM engineers would have never put the stock equipment in this location, as long as you have a fenderliner in place there will be no problem with contamination to the filter.<P><BR>so how is that any better?<P> <BR>Don't mean to jump your case here - just letting you know why I designed the WCF intake like I did, I'm always open for new ideas, as the people in my club ( Fieros West ) know that the design has changed nearly 5 times to make it more effiecient<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<p>[This message has been edited by West Coast Fiero (edited 09-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||09-28-2001||11:58 PM||||people tend to make simple tasks hard.<P>Put it in the fender well , make a splash shield for the opening below the fender well. Don't put your air filter in a hot engine compartment , espically a Fiero. I used a piece of 3.5" ID pipe going into the fenderwell where i removed the factory airbox , thats where the K&N sits , thats where it has sat for 5 YEARS!!!! with a little rubber splash shield to close it off rivited in place and sealed with silicone. I did this on my car , Fierolisa's car and about 10 others over the last 5 years. I wrapped mine with insulated exhaust wrap , according to the pyrometer it's cooler than before. Did the system add Hp ..yeah ..about 3-5hp according to people who have run this type of setup on a  dyno. they also lost 5 to 7lbs of TQ.<P>It's simple. Just use logic. If you have problems with these concepts ..buy the West Coast System, it looks like a nice clean setup.<P>This has been discussed to death.. I'm always amazed at what people come up with for air intakes... Some people used muffler pipe!! with reducers / expanders and all sorts of interesting (ie dumbass)setups<P><BR>I like 1FST2M6's Trunk setup ...very ingenius (sp?)<P>Sorry if I sound snotty , it's late.. and i find this thread funny. <BR>Jonathan M.T McCreery||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroMaster88||09-29-2001||12:25 AM||||N/M.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">..If you wanted to offer suggestions and not ridicule me that would be good..but you cant I guess.  I don't know why I even try sometimes. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by FieroMaster88 (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||DreXteR||09-29-2001||12:38 AM||||Really I mean the intake manifold, from throttle body to lower intake plenum.<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroJoe||09-29-2001||12:51 AM||||if rubber holds heat longer, who cares?  that just means that its going to stay a little hotter when the car is cooling down.  As long as the engine is running, the same amount of heat is going to be put into the metal or into the rubber.  The metal will heat up faster than the rubber, because of the aformentioned differences of coefficients of heat transfer.  And yes, the metal will cool down faster, as long as no more heat (from engine) is being pumped into it.<P>Another thing, about logic.  Yes, it was used.  Have K&N Filter, want cool intake mod.  No extra cash, so, use what you've got.  I was pretty impressed at how clever it was.<P>GM engineering - The stock equipment is sealed off in the location between the 1/4 panel and the engine bay, so you can run around without the fender liner in and you'd not have to worry about crap getting in there (still stock).  If they thought it would be a problem, they would have installed a deflector piece on the bottom, like what slammed put in his to keep crap off his filter.<P>And also, Mr. Jonathan McCreery, just because someone dosent use <B>your</B> setup dosent mean that thiers is a dumbass idea.  Sure, thiers might not work as well as yours might, and maybe only create 2 hp instead of 5, but at least they are still trying to improve the performance of thier car by designing thier own setups, instead of just buying a bolt on part.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sleeper||09-29-2001||12:56 AM||||i have a WCF set-up. pretty easy to install(bout an hour or less). though i bought my own filter, west coast supplies a conical filter. its in the fender well and i have driven in the rain and it doesn't get wet and mud nor dirt gets sprayed up from the road. <P>heres a pic of the filter through the side scoop, HKS mega flow: <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Apr01$01_2_1.jpg"> <P>and heres the tubing. like Slammed i also used insulated exhaust wrap:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ColdAirIntake1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroMaster88||09-29-2001||01:17 AM||||That is a nice setup if you dont mind spending $175 on something you can do yourself for around $50.  But I "have problems with those concepts" so what do I know?<P><p>[This message has been edited by FieroMaster88 (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Leper||09-29-2001||01:17 AM||||Drex, search for what 1fst2m6 has done to his intake manifold. He ported the manfold then milled the gasket areas to shorten the runner length. (there may be more to that, but that's the basics of it.)<P>Fieromaster88, I can understand you wanting to do a cheap modification to get some more HP, but have you ran your k&n with the stock setup to see if there were actual gains by having it the way you do now?<P><BR>West Coast Fiero, I've never heard of the rubber intake hoses expanding with heat to the point where they would restrict any airflow. The rubber also holds more heat instead of transferring it like metal will. A metal inlet setup is still a good aftermarket option though.<P>Slammed's setup is a pretty good setup and is reasonably cheap too.<P>1FST2M6's setup is just plain cool.<P>--------------------<BR>** The preceding was my opinion and if you don't like it. You're just gonna complain anyway so I'm not gonna bother trying to stop you.**||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroMaster88||09-29-2001||01:22 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Leper:<BR><B>Fieromaster88, I can understand you wanting to do a cheap modification to get some more HP, but have you ran your k&n with the stock setup to see if there were actual gains by having it the way you do now?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>We did this setup when I installed the engine.  I really like this better than the stock setup because the air has a lot larger area to travel through, with the stock setup it doesnt.  So no..I didn't try the K&N with the stock setup.  But I dont have a problem with water getting into it.<P><p>[This message has been edited by FieroMaster88 (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||West Coast Fiero||09-29-2001||01:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DreXteR:<BR><B>Really I mean the intake manifold, from throttle body to lower intake plenum.<P>DreX</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>you mean like this?......<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WCF_Intake_(FA51).jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000016||FieroJoe||09-29-2001||01:28 AM||||At highway speeds, most rains still won't hit the air filter.  Oil repels water.  And, has the vacuum theory been proven true?  I have heard some dissention about it before.  The main reason that air gets out of the engine compartment is from convection, not vacuum.<P>EDIT:  C M West, always wondered about that thing.  Since the size of the intake runners is still the stock size, won't all the extra volume of air made by that thing you put in there be in vain, since the runners can only flow as much as they did before?  Has it been dynoed?<P>EDIT II:  Also, that plenum was not designed just to look cool.  By putting that thing in there, havent you now messed with the stock air flow system that was designed into that plenum, and couldn't you possibly run certain cylenders richer or leaner than others?  Has this product been field tested?<BR>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 09-29-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Sleeper||09-29-2001||01:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroMaster88:<BR><B>That is a nice setup if you dont mind spending $175 on something you can do yourself for around $50.  But I "have problems with those concepts" so what do I know?<BR>[This message has been edited by FieroMaster88 (edited 09-29-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>i don't remember paying $175 for it though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> . i think the filter was more...<BR>||||reg||1
