Z||000032||Mickey_Moose||10-01-2001||06:30 PM||||something I came accross while reading in some older performance books...it basically states that but modifing the intake to accept more air it not helping too much due to the MAP. Basically the way it was set up, an engine was run on a dyno and a matrix was constructed of all conditions of enigne speed and load while monitoring the MAP, this matrix was then stored in the ECM prom. IE. the engine was tuned for using the stock air box and filter...the ECM will not take advantage of a free flow system. Now the Chevy 2.8 uses a different system that does not compare the results given by it's MAP sensor to the ECM programming, it just adds more fuel to the mixture with more air.<P>See page 58 of 'Pontiac Fiero Performance Portfolio' book  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Tim<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000033||DreXteR||10-02-2001||12:26 AM||||Mickey_moose - In order to take advantage of the free air flow a chevy ECM will be needed? <P>Also I have another map sensor, is @ least 2 bars.<P>DreX||||reg||5
Z||000034||Leper||10-02-2001||02:31 AM||||What Mickey is saying is that the speed density computer would be lost with the newfound airflow that the intake will give you. He's suggesting that you convert the wiring, sensors and computer over to a Mass air flow computer system found on the non fiero versions of the 2.8 (and probably the 3.4).<BR>You might be able to use other forms of fuel enrichment that I'm sure West Coast will be able to talk about better than I can.||||reg||1
Z||000035||DreXteR||10-02-2001||09:46 AM||||Leper - Thanx fpr the guide.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000036||DreXteR||10-02-2001||10:41 AM||||BTW, Is possible to add another type of sensor to the Fiero ECM?? Lets say if the map sensor is changed for a maf(if the map wouldn be needed) How the ECM will work with it?? Are the same signal?? <P>There are some maf sensors for ford 5.0L that will enable 40% more air flow, Those can be used?<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000037||Mickey_Moose||10-02-2001||06:45 PM||||even if you change the sensor you still have to change the ECM - or at least reprogram the EPROM to take advantage of the air increase<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000038||MooCow||10-02-2001||11:19 PM||||I did the same the same thing that Fieromaster88 did and it made my engine far more cooler. I would hit 220 after sitting for a few minutes in a line.  When I had it on I would barley get to 2 marks in. Also the noise was far better than when it was in stock form.  So no the engine does not suck hot air in.  If you don't believe me do it yourself.  It takes about 10 minutes to do and if you don't like it then change in back in 10 minutes.  But trust me you will notice a difference.<P>Thanks Ken||||reg||2
Z||000039||DreXteR||10-02-2001||11:20 PM||||Mickey_moose - Let say, I have a 2 bar(or higher) map sensor my stock ecm. To take advantage of the more air flow only I need another or recalibrate my chip?<P>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000040||DreXteR||10-03-2001||09:54 AM||||top||||reg||1
Z||000041||DreXteR||10-03-2001||04:04 PM||||Again||||reg||1
Z||000042||Mickey_Moose||10-03-2001||07:44 PM||||actually you shouldn't need to change the MAP sensor at all - it should measure the increase in air flow - it's what the ECM does with it that is the problem (it doesn't take into account that the air flow has been increased)<P>Tim||||reg||1
Z||000043||Shaun41178||10-03-2001||09:44 PM||||Mickey the computer can adjust to more air to a degree.  If it couldnt' then the car would run awefull at anything that GM didn't design it for.  You can go from the mountains of Denver down to sea level and the computer will be able to see that at sea level it has more air and will adjust to that by adding more fuel.  By adding a freer flowing intake it is almost like going closer to sea level for the computer.  The computer can only go as far as its internal parameters allow it but do still adjust.  However it won't be able to understand boost pressure.  Maybe 1 psi boost but that is about it.  Thats where you will need the 2 bar map sensor.<P>Or maybe I just misunderstood what you said?  If so then I apologize. ||||reg||1
Z||000044||DreXteR||10-03-2001||11:34 PM||||What shawn write makes sense. Now, who is resposible to read the air coming from the intake??? Or give the signal to adjust the air/fuel ratio??  How this work??<P><BR>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||5
